WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:06.940
To understand Inertia, we need to look at

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.140
Newton’s first law of motion again.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.620
Here it is.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:15.520
In simple words, this means that

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.180
“Objects tend to keep on doing what they are doing”.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.980
It is the natural tendency of every object

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.740
to resist change in their STATE OF MOTION.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:27.360
If they are at rest, they would prefer staying at rest.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.000
And if they’re moving at constant velocity

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.540
they would want to continue doing so!

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:36.760
This tendency of objects is called nothing but INERTIA!

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:39.320
It’s the natural tendency of an object to

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:41.880
RESIST CHANGE in its state of motion.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.440
Yes, you already knew what inertia is.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:47.720
And yes, Newton’s first law of motion is

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.340
also called the ‘Law of Inertia’.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.820
Do you think all objects have the same inertia?

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.700
All objects resist changes to their state of motion right?

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:00.769
So all should have the same inertia, correct?

00:01:00.769 --> 00:01:02.100
Well... not really,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:06.340
because inertia tells us how strong an object’s tendency is to resist

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.340
change in its state of motion!

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.080
Think of it like this.

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.460
There’s a tiny brick kept on a table,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.560
next to a relatively LARGER and a HEAVIER brick.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:18.320
Which one do you think would be easier to push?

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.240
Without doubt, the tiny brick correct?

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.100
Yes, but why is that?

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.220
It’s because the heavier brick’s tendency

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.920
to resist change in motion is more!

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.360
The heavier the object,

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.500
the more is its tendency to resist the change in motion.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:34.560
The more MASS an object has,

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.780
greater will be its INERTIA.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:40.260
So if we apply equal force to both these bricks,

00:01:40.260 --> 00:01:42.500
the tiny brick will move further as compared

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:43.900
to the larger brick.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.840
The heavier brick has more inertia!

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.960
How can we relate inertia and mass?

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.840
Inertia is the tendency of an object to resist

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.560
changes to its state of motion.

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.700
And the MASS of an object is the

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.420
measure of its INERTIA!

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:01.560
In the next part of this video, we will understand

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.300
this relation with a few interesting examples!

